A1. This holds the record for reproduction. ~ DISK
A5. A leaping amphibian's forget-me-not. ~ WART
A9. Where you can engineer your way thru Boston. ~ TECH
A13. Employees who do this are fired with enthusiasm. ~ IDLE
A14. A prince of a fellow. ~ EMIR
A15. Any old ass can make a hundred bucks here. ~ RODEO
A16. If you pursue this course you will find it has no end. ~ COURS
A18. This has a knack of getting around the feminine element of Japan. ~ OBI
A19. We did not mean for you to get this. ~ ASIDE
A20. A fellow does not always choose wisely when he does this. ~ ELECTS
A22. Old stuff. ~ SPINSTER
A24. Where to hang a horseshoe. ~ HOOF
A26. Drunken beginnings. ~ EDGES
A27. This fellow feels relieved when he is down and out. ~ AVIATOR
A30. He did this when they gave him a square deal. ~ LED
A31. The last word in letter writing (abbr.). ~ PS
A33. What she thought her sugar daddy was. ~ SWEET
A34. Dearer. ~ CUTER
A36. Nut basket. ~ HAT
A37. Just one thing after an-udder. ~ CALF
A38. Lots of women like to keep their dogs in these. ~ MULES
A39. An old Spanish custom. ~ DUTY
A40. When September Morn impresses you only as being this, you're old. ~ ART
A41. This is forever helping to create a scene. ~ EASEL
A42. These play an important part in the old ah me game. ~ SIGHS
A43. This stinger has lost its tail. ~ BE
A44. It is enough to make even a sick man well when he can stand like this. ~ PAT
A45. Give these a good cuff and you'll probably improve them. ~ SLEEVES
A47. An Arizona pool. ~ OASIS
A49. No two people can play this together. ~ SOLO
A50. Prison time. ~ SENTENCE
A53. When you get to this place drop us a lion. ~ AFRICA
A57. With a little tug you will soon be over this. ~ ERIE
A58. Devices most affected by static. ~ EARS
A60. What naval disarmament conferences meet to do. ~ SCRAP
A61. This kind of a note can usually be discounted (abbr.). ~ ANON
A62. It is no wonder that this is a drug on the market when it thrives best on rye. ~ ERGOT
A64. Years and years and years. ~ EONS
A65. You may be able to put this over but it will take lots of canvassing. ~ TENT
A66. Popular surname for Tom. ~ FOOL
A67. And the unkindest cut of all is to put this old sticker last. ~ SNEE

D1. These are indispensable to bone specialists. ~ DICE
D2. My Gawd! ~ IDOL
D3. A lot of. ~ SLUE
D4. Well for crying out loud! ~ KERCHIEF
D5. This always embraces us. ~ WE
D6. A lovely Latin expression. ~ AMO
D7. Bacon had to be separated from these before they could be spared. ~ RIBS
D8. My word! (Apologies to Mr. Nathan). ~ TRIPE
D9. Pitched at night. ~ TOSSED
D10. Shuffles, cuts and deles. ~ EDITS
D11. A regular give-away. ~ CEDE
D12. Earth wrinkler. ~ HOER
D15. Wood life preserver. ~ RANGER
D17. An ermine before the Fall. ~ STOAT
D21. This is usually identified with the first reel. ~ SOT
D23. Turns over without moving. ~ IDLES
D25. All eyes are centered here. ~ FOCUS
D27. He stole a kiss before she became this. ~ AWARE
D28. This helped to make a name for one of New York's governors. ~ VELT
D29. These may still be good no matter how badly they are broken. ~ RULES
D31. These are worn by dogs. ~ PATHS
D32. Where footballs were originally manufactured (possessive). ~ STYS
D33. The tick of this was O. K. and it was a good timekeeper, but the Union condemned it because it would not strike. ~ SCAB
D35. Keeps a secret (feminine). ~ TELLS
D36. This is immense. ~ HUGE
D38. You will always find at least one ham and plenty of roles here. ~ MATINEE
D39. Renovates. ~ DIVORCES
D41. A girl with an inferiority complex will rarely be found at this. ~ EASE
D42. Where I disease usually strikes (add "s"). ~ SELFS
D44. This ought to be perfectly apparent. ~ PATENT
D46. The end of leisure and the beginning of outdoor athletics. ~ EOA
D47. This is least obnoxious when pickled or stewed. ~ ONION
D48. A conspicuous necker. ~ SCARF
D50. Pullman officials deny that this gives the right to practice berth control. ~ SEAT
D51. A marine highflier. ~ ERNE
D52. This is rather consequential. ~ ERGO
D54. A smooth press agent. ~ IRON
D55. This can be used to smarten up the younger generation. ~ CANE
D56. The end of the church. ~ APSE
D59. This is bound together in the northern U. S. by many strong ties and has thousands of sleepers who never snore. ~ SOO
D63. Here's where you will get the limit (abbr.). ~ TL


